    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:40:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Alam Kuh: Granite Walls and Glaciers | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Alam Kuh’s place in the Alborz, its glacier landscape, and firsthand accounts of climbing and international mountain exchanges."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mountain-and-massif",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A peak within a larger mountain landscape",
        "content": "Alam Kuh belongs to the Takht-e Soleyman massif, a closely connected group of high summits in the Alborz Mountains of northern Iran. A glacier inventory published in 2009 identifies it as the massif’s highest peak. Understanding the mountain therefore means looking beyond an isolated summit: adjoining ridges, shaded slopes, and glacier basins form the setting in which both climbers and researchers work. [s1]\n\nPublished elevations differ. The glacier inventory gives 4,850 metres, while the title of an American Alpine Journal report uses 4,805 metres. These figures should remain attributed to their sources rather than being presented as interchangeable survey results. Neither inspected publication resolves the difference through a common vertical datum or a new summit survey. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "glacier-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ice beneath the ridges",
        "content": "The [glacier inventory](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/annals-of-glaciology/article/new-glacier-inventory-of-iran/18BCB89BEEBD29F27C78C0C2C4BAE36C) places much of the massif’s ice on north-facing slopes. Its mapping distinguishes Alamchal, Patakht, and Takht-e Soleyman as connected glacier segments, illustrating how a single ice system can acquire several names. Rock debris covers part of the ice surface, complicating the impression a photograph or distant view might give. [s1]\n\nThe inventory is a historical research baseline, not a measurement of conditions in 2026. Its Takht-e Soleyman mapping uses 2005 imagery; glacier names and mapped boundaries must be read alongside that observation date. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "climbing-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A wall with an international climbing history",
        "content": "Alam Kuh’s granite north face has drawn visiting climbers into contact with Iranian mountaineers. In a [firsthand account published in 1999](https://publications.americanalpineclub.org/articles/12199913300/Alum-Kuh-A-Climbing-Appointment-with-the-Islamic-Republic-of-Iran), British climber Kath Pyke describes a September 1998 expedition and an ascent with Glenda Huxter of an existing Iranian-German route. The visit also included skills exchanges with Iranian women climbers. [s2]\n\nPyke’s account makes clear why the wall cannot be understood simply as a rock-climbing destination. Her team encountered loose material above the climbing and substantial rockfall around the approaches. She also recorded concerns about discarded equipment and rubbish near mountain huts, alongside roads and mining. These are dated eyewitness observations; they establish issues raised during that expedition, without measuring their subsequent extent. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exchange-and-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Shared knowledge in demanding terrain",
        "content": "A later [American Alpine Journal exchange report](https://publications.americanalpineclub.org/articles/12201220100/Asia-Middle-East-Iran-Alam-Kuh-4805m-and-Damavand-5610m-AAC-Exchange), published in 2012, describes American Alpine Club members climbing with Alpine Club of Iran hosts. It distinguishes the lower North Patakht granite buttress from the main wall and records snow and wind limiting climbing. The report also describes shared crack-climbing practice, showing that the exchange involved teaching and partnership as well as ascents. [s3]\n\nRead together, these accounts present Alam Kuh as a meeting place for mountain knowledge. They also explain why historical route reports have limits: a successful ascent documents an encounter with particular conditions, not a dependable description of what another party will find. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
