    "description": "Sabalan is a volcanic mountain west of Ardabil in northwestern Iran, known for its summit crater lake and high-altitude ice. Its slopes connect volcanic landforms with grazing landscapes, mineral springs, and regional tourism.",
    "tags": ["Sabalan", "Northwestern Iran", "Volcanoes", "Crater lakes", "Mountain ecology"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:40:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:40:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Sabalan: Volcano, Crater Lake and Highland Ecology | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Sabalan’s volcanic landscape, summit lake, mountain vegetation, and the distinction between a UNESCO tentative listing and inscription."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "volcanic-mountain",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A volcanic landmark of northwestern Iran",
        "content": "Sabalan rises west of Ardabil, inland from the Caspian Sea. The [Smithsonian Global Volcanism Program](https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=232002) classifies it as a stratovolcano, a composite volcanic landform, and identifies andesite and basaltic andesite among its principal rock types. Its profile records glaciers and rock glaciers as part of the mountain landscape. [s1]\n\nIts elevation is not consistently reported. The Smithsonian lists 4,784 metres, while Iran’s submission to UNESCO gives 4,820 metres. The discrepancy is left explicit here because the inspected records do not reconcile their measurement methods. A precise height should therefore be accompanied by its source. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "eruption-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What is known about its volcanic history",
        "content": "The Smithsonian’s headline classification places Sabalan’s last known eruption in the Pleistocene. Its discussion also acknowledges a published suggestion of activity continuing into the Holocene, the geological epoch following the Pleistocene, while its eruptive-history section says it knows of no Holocene eruptions. This uneven evidence does not support assigning the mountain a precise last-eruption date. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters: a broad geological classification is different from a dated eruption record. Likewise, the absence of weekly activity reports in the database is not itself a comprehensive assessment of volcanic hazards. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "lake-and-recognition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The crater lake and the heritage proposal",
        "content": "A freshwater lake occupies the summit crater. Iran’s [UNESCO Tentative List submission](https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5218/), dated 9 August 2007, presents the lake and the mountain’s volcanic scenery as central features of the proposed natural property. It also describes mineral and thermal springs on the southern slopes and their role in tourism. [s2]\n\nThis record documents a proposal by Iran’s heritage organization. A tentative listing is a preliminary step, not a World Heritage inscription or independent confirmation of every claim in the submission. UNESCO explicitly assigns responsibility for the description to the submitting state. The submission’s promotional comparisons and broad assertions of intact scenery should consequently be read as nomination arguments, rather than as a recent ecological assessment. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "slopes-and-livelihoods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From cultivated slopes to high mountain ground",
        "content": "The [Encyclopaedia Iranica account](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/sabalan-mountain/) describes an ecological progression from steppe and agricultural land to subalpine and alpine meadows, followed by increasingly sparse vegetation and exposed rock. Altitude divides the massif into different environments; the summit alone cannot represent the whole mountain. [s3]\n\nThat account connects the higher forelands with summer grazing used by village livestock keepers and Shahsevan pastoralists. It also describes the thermal springs as destinations for visitors. These uses show how Sabalan’s significance extends beyond volcanic geology: snow, pasture, and warm groundwater support different relationships with the same massif. The source is a historical geographical synthesis, however, and does not establish present livestock numbers, visitor totals, or the condition of individual springs. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
